<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Bold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1433">1433</a>–<a href="/years/1477">1477</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles, Duke of Burgundy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Bold was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1467, ruling the Burgundian lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>. He sacked rebellious Liège in 1468, bought Guelders in 1473 and sought a royal crown to link his territories. His wars with the Swiss and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> ended in defeat, and he was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> in 1477, after which his inheritance passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold.jpg" alt="Charles the Bold" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rogier van der Weyden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_the_Bold_1460.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Martin was born on 10 November 1433 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, the only child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> and his third wife, Isabella of Portugal, to survive infancy. His father made him a knight of the Golden Fleece and count of Charolais. From 1454 to 1464 Philip excluded him from power. In his youth Charles was known for his chivalric ambitions and his admiration for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He developed an intense rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a>, who became king on 22 July 1461, and in 1465 formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-the-public-weal">League of the Public Weal</a>, a coalition of French feudal lords, and led them in a war against the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> died on 15 June 1467, and Charles formally became duke fourteen days later. His joyous entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> provoked unrest, and the following January he forced the mayors to ask his pardon and abolished the city's governmental rights. He inherited a vast but fragmented collection of territories from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and wanted to turn them into an independent kingdom.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> in October 1473 he met the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick III, hoping to be recognised as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and to betroth his daughter Mary to the emperor's son Maximilian. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-york">Margaret of York</a> to secure an English alliance. A patron of illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, he kept a court noted for its chivalric culture and etiquette. Between 1468 and 1473 he issued military ordinances, creating in 1471 a standing army of 1,250 lances fournies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, handgunners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> against an alliance of Swiss, German and Alsatian powers. His siege of Neuss lasted from July 1474 to June 1475 and cost him heavily. In 1476 he was defeated by the Swiss at Grandson and at Morat. When René II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> on 6 October 1476, Charles besieged it through the winter. On 5 January 1477, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a>, the Count of Campobasso's Italian mercenaries having deserted to René, Charles was killed. His body was found two days later on the frozen river, and René had him buried in the Saint-George collegiate church. In Artois some refused to believe he was dead.</p><p>Three weeks later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> invaded Burgundian territory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> rebelled, and Liège and Guelders sought independence. His death led to the collapse of the Burgundian State, since he had no legitimate male heir, and he was succeeded by his only child, Mary. His German enemies called him the Grand Turk of the West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles the Bold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles the Bold</b> created standing companies of ordinance in 1471 and a powerful artillery train, but his army was routed by the Swiss Confederates at the Battle of Grandson and the Battle of Morat in 1476, and he was killed at the Battle of Nancy on 5 January…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy’s greatest military moment came under René II, whose lands lay across the corridor <b>Charles the Bold</b> of Burgundy needed to link his territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Burgundian Wars they defeated <b>Charles the Bold</b> of Burgundy at the Battle of Grandson and the Battle of Murten in 1476 and, as allies of Duke René II of Lorraine, at the Battle of Nancy in 1477, where Charles was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Liège rose again, was defeated at the Battle of Brustem in 1467 and, after a final rising, was taken and sacked by <b>Charles the Bold</b> in 1468; according to tradition six hundred men of Franchimont made a desperate night attack on the duke’s camp before the city fell.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard Taruskin noted that it was a favourite of <b>Charles the Bold</b> and suggested it may have been written for him, but researchers have shown that it was in use before he became Duke of Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Charles the Bold</b> died fighting Swiss and Lorrainer troops outside Nancy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Nancy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1461" aria-label="Dijon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4668 504 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles the Bold"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5118 774h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5118 774h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1461">What was here in 1461? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
